        "input": {
          "instructions": "Professional, concise, and paced for an introductory course audience.",
          "text": "Real systems lose energy through damping, so their response does not grow without bound. Engineers can add damping, change stiffness or mass, or keep operating speeds away from a troublesome natural frequency. The practical lesson is simple: resonance is not automatically failure, but ignoring the frequency relationship can turn a small repeating force into a large motion."
        },
